      "openalex_id": "W93531884",
      "doi": null,
      "title": "The Alcoholics of War: Experiencing Chemical and Ideological Drunkenness in Emilio Lussu's Un Anno Sull'altipiano",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Umberto Rossi",
          "openalex_id": "A5049136496",
          "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9785-4237"
        }
      ],
      "publication_year": 2005,
      "publication_date": "2005-09-01",
      "abstract": "This article applies the reflections on experience and narrative found in Walter Benjamin's Storyteller to a popular Italian World War I novel, Emilio Lussu's Un anno sull'altipiano. The essay focuses on alcohol abuse repeatedly depicted in the novel as a component that can lead to a dissolution of traditional narrative forms. ********** Any attempt to discuss modern war narratives unavoidably entails discussing the question of experience, a question that has indissolubly tied to narration in Walter Benjamin's 1936 essay Storyteller [Der Erzahler]. In the first section of Benjamin's essay, we find the famous passage on war experience that asks: Was it not noticeable at the end of the war that men returned from the battlefield grown silent--not richer but poorer in communicable experience? (84). A slightly different version of this passage was already present in his 1933 essay Erfahrung und Armut, suggesting that the triangular connection among the Great War, narration, and the issue of experience was a long-term concern for Benjamin (214). Though war (and war narratives) is just an example of the storytelling art's decline, it is a relevant one. Benjamin lists other instances of historical events that have devalued experience, and two of them (position warfare and the war of materials) are features of World War I; the others (inflation and the amorality of ruling classes) are connected to it. Benjamin tells us that modernity has caused the decline of storytelling, and that modern warfare (especially World War I) is the paradigmatic manifestation of modernity. But in this first part of Storyteller, we find a paradox: those who took part in the war, who have personally been there, cannot really tell the war because they have not experienced it. So we should ask ourselves whether narration is just something that comes after experience, that is, an effect of experience. Perhaps the tragedy of veterans' impotence to experience is consummated in the very moment they are unable to tell their stories, since what we call experience might be no more than a side effect of narration. In other words: only when we configure experience in a narration are we really experiencing something, a position close to Paul Ricoeur's philosophical reflections on narratives and time in Temps et recit. If we survey the field of written narratives, we see that Benjamin's growing silent was definitely not a universal condition, at least from a phenomenological point of view. Many war memoirs were published in the period between 1915-1922: some of them are classics of war literature, such as Ernst Junger's Storms of Steel and Henri Barbousse's Under Fire. E. M. Remarque published his All Quiet on the Western Front in 1929 with overwhelming success (Eksteins 276), and this was followed by a second flood of war memoirs, Kriegsbucher, war novels, etc. (Stickelberger-Eder 16-33). To this second wave belong such masterpieces as Hemingway's 1929 A Farewell to Arms and Ludwig Renn's Krieg in 1928. Yet Benjamin seems to mistrust these texts, because in 1933 and in 1936 he contrasts the deluge of war books with the dearth of experience in them--an experience Benjamin resolutely connects to verbal communication: What ten years later was poured out in the flood of war books [Kriegsbucher] was anything but the experience that goes from to mouth (Storyteller 84). Those narratives are thus struck by an interdict: they are inauthentic forms of narration. Such a negative judgement on modern war narrative's capability to tell the war can also be found in Theodor W. Adorno's writings (see Minima Moralia 63). And, what is even more striking, similar doubts are found in the critical writings of other scholars who have dealt with the theme of the Great War narratives and writers, yet do not mention Benjamin or Adorno: Paul Fussell, Modris Eksteins, John Cuickshank, Evelyn Cobley, Wolfgang Kaempfer. \u2026",
      "cited_by_count": 0,
